2610.02183v1,Single-Particle Spectral Estimation,"As quasiparticle spectra feature ubiquitously in downstream
materials calculations, and with SPICES being simple and practical to execute, we expect that
SPICES is a promising route to quantum applications in materials simulation.
1 Introduction
In this work we consider the notion of afree spectrum– wherein the eigenvalues of a Hamiltonian
are decomposable into distinct sums of a polynomial number of “single particle” energies{Ei}. More recently, mappings to free fermions
were discovered to have played a role in the proof of the sensitivity conjecture [17, 18]. We expect
that our characterization, by revealing hidden structure in these settings, may find application in all
of these areas. We
note that for simplicity our structural promise(1) keeps only the leading term of(6): the Landau
interaction fij couples quasiparticles to one another, whereas we require the protected modes to
decouple – we aim to address generalizations of this in future work.","2 Discussion
We suspect that the rigorous promises that SPICES requires can likely be relaxed in practice. We
use logarithmic inverse temperature dependence to guarantee these, but it is not cleara priorithat
this requirement is ultimately necessary. In experiments one is often satisfied with coarse-grained
features of the single-particle energy density, such as bandgaps, rather than individual energies. We
would find it plausible that a more forgiving scaling may suffice in this relaxed setting. Additionally,
our algorithmic results assume controlled evolution and Gibbs preparation at inverse temperature
O(logM ), and the hardness result shows that the number operator cannot be straightforwardly
removed from the access model. It would be interesting to identify if certain physical systems allow
for a weaker form of access toNfor the same task.
6
There are a number of directions we intend to elaborate on in near term future work.
Firstly, we have been engaged in testing numerically the SPICES method and have seen that it
performs well. The present paper is meant to present the theoretical foundations of the method with
a practical survey to follow.
Secondly, the SPICES protocol may be generalized to a larger family of good quantum numbers
beyond total chargeN. For example these techniques may be extended to total momentum in order
to extract single particle momenta, i.e. band structure. From this perspective we may think of
the SPICES protocol as a technique for identifying where global symmetries decompose into local
symmetries.
Thirdly, the SPICES estimator can be used as a primitive in a swept estimator, wherein the radial
values of the contours are swept across an energy domain, yielding a regularized energy distribution.
We have seen that this works well numerically – which suggests that the condition that roots be
bounded away from the contours may potentially be relaxed in this setting.
Fourthly, we would like to understand better where in practice we can expect the benefits of
the SPICES approach to be most impactful and how the performance of the method compares to
and can supplement Green’s functions for practical applications. In line with this, we would like to
understand in detail how the higher-order parameters in the Landau Fermi Liquid hypothesis given
in equation 6 impact the estimator produced by SPICES.
Finally, we would like to show that SPICES can also be employed to witness theabsenceof a
free model, improving its usefulness as a diagnostic method.
AcknowledgementsWe thank Raul Santos, Toby Cubitt, Evan Sheridan, and Glenn Jones for
helpful discussions throughout this project.
Author ContributionsJK formulated the research direction. SF and JK provided technical
leadership. AC, CD, SF and JK developed the SPICES algorithm. AC, JK, CD, SF, YH, CM
developed the proofs of correctness and noise robustness. YH and AC developed the proofs of DQC1
hardness.
AI Disclosure.The formulation of the research direction, problem statement, algorithm and
hardness construction was done by the authors. Proof sketches were formulated by the authors with
AI used to assist in proof completion. AI was used in optimizing the bounds and resource estimates
for the algorithm. AI was used to assist with paper drafting, editing and the design of figures and
diagrams.
Note added.During the final stages of drafting of this document we became aware of contempo-
raneous work employing similar algorithmic techniques to the ones used here in Ref. [35]. We note
that our setting is distinct from this one in that we allow for the presence of an interacting spectator
Hamiltonian and includes models which are free-fermion in an unknown basis.
