This equation suggests taking a Fourier transform int in order to estimate the energies whose
roots lie inA from the peaks in the spectrum. This idea provides the correct intuition, but we need
to address some technical caveats to make this work.
Foremost, if we know thekth root moment for timest∈ [0,t max], we can only learn the single-
particle energies to precision aboutk−1/tmax, or about1/tmax for fixedk >0. Thus, if our goal is
an algorithm that learnsEj toarbitraryprecision, we need to be able to observe root moments at
arbitrarily long times.
The next caveat is that the boundary ofA must be a union of centered circles. Otherwise,A
will not generally contain the same zeros for all times. Indeed,
|e−(β+it)Ej|= e−βEj
is independent oft, while the phase rotates at angular velocity−Ej. For a general region, a zeroe−sEj
will fluctuate inside and outside of the region as time evolves, essentially convolving its contribution
to the spectrum with a window function. This will set a lower bound on the precision with which
one can inferEj due to spectral broadening (even if shot noise were no concern).
The third and worst caveat is that a zero fluctuating through the boundary necessarily implies
that there is a timet when the zero isonthe boundary, which is expressly forbidden in the definition
of the root moments and destroys the ability of any shot-based estimator to achieve good relative
accuracy at these crossing times.
Because of these considerations we restrict ourselves to regions bounded by centered circles. We
will use discs and annuli, with annuli represented as differences of two discs with opposite boundary
orientations. These regions are merely sufficient for our purposes, not necessary. However, we do
not know of any good reason to consider more general regions, and this choice greatly simplifies our
subsequent calculations.
