produce order-one radial motion on the time scale1/|λ|. In particular, when the spectator is only
weakly separated from the window, the finite-time qualification cannot be removed for general
interacting spectators. Thus, despite the commutation relation[VO,HO] = 0, the interaction induces
a change in the root moment.
The example is a weak-gap phenomenon. For every realt we have|1− e−sλ|≤ 1 + e−βλ < 2,
so by(82) both roots satisfy|ζ±(s)|< (1 +
√
2) e−βE at all times. Ifβ(E− Ωmax)≥log (1 +
√
2),
neither root can ever reach the inner contour, and the mechanism above disappears. We therefore
make no claim about the necessity of a finite horizon in the logarithmic-gap regime of Corollary 37.
