We apply Lemma 63 in the proof of Proposition 23 with ε′ = ϵ/(4L) ∈ (0, 1
8). Then
⌈6/ε′⌉ =⌈24L/ϵ⌉ = T, and κ0 = 10−3ϵ/(4Llog (4L/ϵ)), so the requirement κ≤κ 0 holds for
κ = cϵ/ (Llog (4L/ϵ))as soon as c≤ 1/4000. This is the sense in whichc is a sufficiently small
universal constant in that proposition. It is therefore enough to findanyprobability measure
consistent with the observed coefficients to the required accuracy.
There are two obstacles to expressing this search as a finite-dimensional linear program. First,ρ
may be atomic, so a direct piecewise-constant approximation need not be accurate. Convolution
with a Jackson kernel movesρ by onlyO(1/n)in Wasserstein distance while producing a smooth
66
density. Second, the space of smooth densities is still infinite dimensional. Partitioning the torus
intoK cells and using the mass in each cell as a variable gives a finite probability simplex. The
scale K =O(n2/κ)is chosen because the Jackson-smoothed density has Lipschitz constantO(n2),
