.","Conclusions




In this work, our primary focus has been to demonstrate the potential of augmenting nanophotonic devices with programmable nonlinearity. We believe one of the most important contributions of our work is to point toward a new development and manufacturing paradigm beyond the conventional one-device-one-function approach, which could eventually enable truly scalable integrated nonlinear photonics. A programmable device can be deployed as a “blank slate” that then inversely designs its own function, in situ, based on real-time feedback from the environment in which it operates. This would not only enable the robust engineering of complex functions with 100% yield but also allow the device to remain fully adaptive to changing environments and demands, potentially transcending nonlinear nanophotonics beyond its present regime of small circuit depths toward systems of much greater scale.






As the technology matures, the exact means of realizing programmable nonlinearity may well evolve. Our approach based on a photoconductor and free-space programming illumination was convenient for a proof of concept, but to maximize integrability, it may turn out to be advantageous to employ lithographically defined electrodes and integrated circuitry to control the bias electric fields. Unlike conventional nonlinear nanophotonics, which relies on specialized materials, all of these approaches could in principle be implemented within complementary metal-oxide-semiconductor (CMOS)-compatible fabrication, further underscoring the potential for scalability 
[
58
, 
57
]
.






Although much development is needed before reaching that stage, we emphasize that this would inherently be a paradigm with implications beyond any single application. The one-device-many-functions paradigm means that a wide range of nonlinear nanophotonic systems, spanning different applications and contexts, can be built on the same general-purpose device. In analogy with the transistor, this large market could justify the extensive manufacturing and design optimization that might be prohibitive for more specialized nonlinear nanophotonic devices. The proof-of-concept results presented here offer a glimpse of what this new paradigm for nonlinear nanophotonics could look like.










