This population balance on pore space yields a mass transfer model which achieves two key goals:




1.




In amorphous material (i.e. susceptible to microcollapse during lyophilization) and with uniform formulation and freezing, the population balance model can predict mass transfer resistance across different drying conditions, so long as it is fit to at least one conservative and one aggressive case.




2.




With separate measurements of (the moments of) pore size distributions in a non-microcollapsing material, the population balance model is capable of matching several experiments with a single value of the tortuosity-like parameter 
C
C
.










Future work can establish a rigorous functional form for the microcollapse rate constant 
k
k
 as a function of temperature, as well as explore a more rigorous expression relating moments of the pore size distribution to 
R
p
R_{p}
.






