Numerical studies of a surface ion-trap with variable geometry




We now apply our noise-simulation method to the task of ion-trap design: We aim to minimize the total electric-field noise seen by an ion in the trap and to examine the relative importance of noise due to metals and noise due to dielectrics.","Conclusion




We have introduced a FEA method for analyzing electric-field noise in ion traps. In contrast to earlier work 
[
12
]
, this method takes into account not only dielectrics but also conducting materials, allowing us to make quantitative predictions of the electric-field noise and ion heating rate in a given trap. The method has been applied to a surface ion trap with an electrically floating metallic wire, yielding agreement between model and experiment when the dielectric loss tangent is a free parameter. The floating wire allowed us to characterize the noise due to the dielectric trap substrate, as the wire propagates the electric-field noise from the substrate to the ion, which then dominates over other noise sources. Furthermore, we have simulated the heating rate of an ion in a typical surface ion trap along all three trap axes, varying the trench depth, trench width, and undercut width and identifying parameter regimes in which the trap design fails to shield the ion sufficiently from dielectric components.






For a given trap design, this method makes it possible to calculate heating rates that can be directly compared with measured data, which will allow researchers to identify strengths and weaknesses in existing ion traps, including materials choice, fabrication approaches, floating electrodes, and cleaning methods. For example, the heating rates that we determined in our four studies of a trap geometry similar to that of Ref. 
[
7
]
 (Figs. 
4
 and 
5
) are comparable to heating rates observed in that trap after in-situ surface cleaning. This suggests that in-situ cleaning can be successful in reducing electric-field noise to a level that is given by the intrinsic losses of the underlying materials.
More generally, the simulated values for electric-field noise provide a lower bound on what is achievable with a given ion-trap geometry 
[
18
]
. Applying this method as part of an iterative design process will allow ion-trap geometries to be tailored such that the bulk materials contribution to particle heating is below a target value and well understood, offering a useful tool for future quantum simulators and fault-tolerant quantum computers 
[
1
]
.






