2610.01008v1,"Modeling Shipping Emissions: Machine Learning, Engineering, and Policy Counterfactuals","Whether the correction carries over to a counterfactual rests on the assumption that the relation of its retained inputs to fuel consumption does not shift with speed, which cannot be verified outside the training data.","8 
Conclusion




This paper develops and evaluates combinations of 
ML
 and theory-based structure to predict ship fuel consumption and its response to counterfactual changes in sailing speed. Both quantities are essential for shipping emissions policy. We assess aggregate predictions against reported consumption and compare estimated speed responses with an engineering benchmark. Several of our models outperform those used for official estimates on at least one of these criteria, while performing comparably on the other.






In the 2021 test sample, every estimated model predicts aggregate fuel consumption within a few percent of reported totals, improving upon the calibrated engineering calculation. Yet the models imply markedly different speed responses. For dry bulk ships, pure 
ML
 and the unrestricted structural regression yield responses far below the benchmark. Calibration-anchored models generally track the benchmark response closely, though they retain some aggregate overprediction. In the main specification, two simple models without 
ML
 combine low aggregate prediction bias with responses close to the benchmark: the rescaled engineering calculation and the structural regression constrained at the engine’s design load, both estimated without an intercept. This restriction sets the slope of the efficiency curve to zero at the design load rather than leaving its slope and curvature unconstrained.






The illustrative cost-benefit exercise demonstrates why the distinction between predictive accuracy and speed responsiveness matters. Among the five models considered, the calibrated engineering calculation and the constrained structural regression imply positive net benefits from the imposed speed reductions, whereas the selected pure 
ML
 models and the unrestricted structural regression imply negative net benefits. Models with similarly small aggregate prediction errors can thus yield opposite cost-benefit conclusions under identical valuation assumptions.






We draw three methodological lessons. First, the response used in a counterfactual should be evaluated separately from statistical fit. Second, the separable hybrids studied here offer a transparent way to prevent a flexible correction from altering a specified structural property. Excluding speed-related variables from the correction leaves it unchanged when speed changes and other inputs are held fixed. Consequently, the multiplicative hybrid preserves the structural component’s ship-level speed elasticity, while the additive hybrid preserves its speed derivative in levels. This property neither guarantees small bias in levels nor validates the preserved response, and transferring the correction to a counterfactual requires an additional invariance assumption. Third, distance in a model’s feature space can flag departures from the training distribution. Our diagnostic serves as a warning indicator rather than a test of counterfactual validity: a small distance neither establishes that an observation lies within the training support nor guarantees agreement with the benchmark.






For emissions accounting, the same issues arise when models are applied beyond annual totals. Our replication of the 
OECD
 fuel-efficiency model matches reported totals closely but exhibits essentially no speed response for dry bulk ships. Moving from ship-years to ship-months or to global route aggregates can likewise place prediction inputs far outside the training distribution. These applications call for assessing both the relevant input responses and the conditions under which predictions are transferred.






Several limitations qualify these findings. Estimation relies on three years of reported consumption for two vessel types on voyages to, from, and between European Economic Area ports, retaining ship-years whose tracked and reported distances agree within the specified tolerance. The engineering response is a maintained benchmark rather than an observed counterfactual. In addition, the unrestricted estimates reject the design-load restriction imposed by the constrained regression. Accounting for wave resistance reduces the constrained regression’s response to speed reductions to roughly one-third of its uncorrected magnitude, while leaving its response to speed increases largely unchanged. Furthermore, supplementary exercises addressing extrapolation, measurement error, and structural nonlinearity do not fully explain departures from the benchmark. Finally, the cost-benefit exercise evaluates speed changes while holding distance, fleet composition, and trade fixed, capturing neither operators’ behavioral responses to specific policies nor general equilibrium adjustments.






The central implication is that predictive performance and the credibility of a counterfactual response require separate assessment.",cues
