Conclusions




In this work, we have introduced a hyperbolic relaxation system for the simulation of kinetic equations in the low-Mach number limits. By employing a micro-macro decomposition of the BGK model and appropriate simplifications, we derived a closed moment-based system whose hyperbolic core simplifies Grad’s linear moment framework while bypassing the high-dimensional discretization of velocity space.






Theoretically, we established the structural stability of the model in the incompressible limit via symmetrizability and proved its consistency with the INS equations. To bridge the continuum and rarefied regimes, we developed a high-order AP numerical framework combining IMEX RK schemes with high-order spatial reconstructions (WENO and central differences) under established stability constraints. In the asymptotic limit (
ϵ
→
0
\epsilon\to 0
), the scheme successfully degenerates into a consistent projection method for the INS equations on collocated grids.






Numerical experiments in 1D and 2D validate our theoretical findings across distinct physical regimes. In the continuum-dominated regime (noting that thresholds such as 
ε
≤
