−
3
\varepsilon>10^{-3}
), the expected growing deviations reflect the truncated moment hierarchy of the reduced-order system outside the hydrodynamic limit.






Crucially, our continuous moment-based approach achieves consistent asymptotic convergence toward the high-fidelity reference solution as 
ε
→
0
\varepsilon\to 0
, completely bypassing the computational bottleneck of high-dimensional velocity space discretizations. Furthermore, the relaxation framework is naturally formulated for and seamlessly applicable to arbitrary spatial dimensions without requiring specialized stencil or velocity discrete constructions. Coupled with computational speedups of several orders of magnitude in stiff regimes compared to full kinetic solvers, the proposed framework provides a robust and cost-effective tool for multiscale fluid dynamics, while remaining subject to the model-validity qualifications discussed earlier in this work.






Looking ahead, several promising avenues for future research emerge from the results presented in this work. One primary direction involves extending the current relaxation framework to incorporate more complex collision operators, such as the full Boltzmann equation or ES-BGK models, to more accurately capture physical phenomena involving non–constant Prandtl numbers.
Furthermore, to improve the model’s fidelity in more rarefied regimes (i.e., for larger values of 
ε
\varepsilon
), the system could be extended to include higher kinetic moments, akin to Grad’s extended moment systems. This would systematically reduce the truncation error of the physical model outside the hydrodynamic limit.
Additionally, the development and implementation of high–order AP boundary conditions for complex geometries will be crucial for applying this methodology to practical engineering problems. We refer to 
[
56
]
 for investigations in this direction for moment systems. Finally, the significant reduction in computational cost offered by our relaxation system makes it an ideal candidate for Uncertainty Quantification (UQ) studies.",cues
