The principal point that we tried to clarify here is that the fine
characteristics of the FS shape can significantly affect the
electron properties of conventional 3D metals and other
materials such as Q2D layered conductors. The effect of the
local geometric features of FSs (nearly cylindrical and/or
nearly paraboloidal segments, flattened regions, and others)
can be manifested under certain conditions, when the
conduction electron response to an external disturbance is
mostly determined by a small segment of the FS. This is the
case with the anomalous skin effect and magnetic quantum
oscillations of thermodynamic variables. In both cases, the
response of a metal to an external disturbance is mainly
formed by the charge carriers associated with narrow strips
on the FS. When the FS curvature vanishes at these ‘effective’
strips, the frequency dependences of the surface impedance of
a metal can noticeably change and the magnetic quantum
oscillations can be enhanced. Local flattenings of the FS can
significantly affect the commensurability magnetoacoustic
oscillations described in Section 4. Observable effects arising
due to the FS curvature anomalies at certain points can occur
in truly low-dimensional conductors, such as the two-
dimensional electron gas created in GaAs=AlGaAs super-
structures, if the electron density is periodically modulated by
applying an additional static electric field [116, 117].
The entire analysis presented in this review is based on the
phenomenological models of the FS geometry over the
relevant segments. The reason for the preference given to
these models over the results of electron band structure
computations is that even advanced computational methods
cannot guarantee that such fine features as flattening points
or narrow, nearly cylindrical strips are not missed in the
process of FS reconstruction. In this paper, we concentrate on
the analysis of possible experimental manifestations of such
features. For instance, it was shown in Section 5 that the
angular dependences of the magnitudes of magnetic quantum
oscillations in Q2D metals predicted by Yamaji [131] radically
differ from the angular dependences of similar characteristics
that can appear due to the presence of nearly cylindrical strips
on the relevant FSs. Phenomenological models remain
extremely useful in these analyses because they allow
relatively easily clarifying the nature and origin of various
phenomena and identifying those that arise due to the FS
local geometry.
We thank M V Sadovskii and I I Mazin for the helpful
discussions. Also, the author is sincerely grateful to all the
colleagues with whom she collaborated during the years given
to the ‘fermiological’studies, and to G M Zimbovsky for his
help in preparing the manuscript.",cues
2610.00794v1,Long-Range Machine Learning Interatomic Potentials for Defect Energetics in SrTiO$_3$,"The diversity of these approaches reflects both the importance and the open nature of the problem. In this work, we seek to understand the performance of MLIPs for defects in oxide materials, using strontium titanate (SrTiO
3
) as a prototypical example.","Conclusion




Overall, we have presented a systematic benchmark of long-range machine learning interatomic potentials, namely MACE-LES, MACE-POLAR, and LOREM, progressing from idealised synthetic point-charge systems to DFT data for strontium titanate with Sr–O Schottky vacancy pairs.






In the synthetic benchmark, MACE-LES emerged as the strongest performer, demonstrating that explicit long-range energy evaluation in reciprocal space confers a meaningful advantage when the interaction is purely electrostatic. In the DFT data benchmark, all three long-range models showed strong generalisation across training regimes, producing correctly shaped and quantitatively reliable minimum energy pathways for oxygen vacancy migration, yielding activation barriers in good agreement with literature values. This is a notable extrapolation, as the training data comprised only bulk MD configurations and relaxed (endpoint) Schottky defect structures, with no intermediate migration geometries resembling the transition-state configurations sampled along the NEB path. By contrast, purely short-range models such as standard MACE remain fundamentally limited by their real-space cutoff, and this limitation becomes especially acute in defected structures where charged defects introduce slowly decaying electrostatic interactions.






However, there exist several important caveats with these findings. The synthetic benchmark, while instructive for isolating electrostatic behaviour, is by design an extreme test case: few real materials present interactions as purely long-range as formal point charges, and a model’s underperformance on this task should not be taken as disqualification for most applications. Similarly, the DFT benchmark metrics should not be interpreted as straightforward improvements over the synthetic case. The complexity of the DFT total energy, encompassing short-range repulsion, exchange-correlation, dispersion, and ionic self-energies, dilutes the electrostatic signal and could reduce the associated errors with electrostatics.






Looking forward, several extensions of this work can be considered. Phonon calculations and the evaluation of finite-temperature thermodynamic properties would test whether the long-range accuracy demonstrated in static benchmarks carries through to dynamical quantities. More complex defect structures, including higher vacancy concentrations and non-neutral supercells with explicit compensating charges, would probe the models’ robustness under conditions directly relevant to electrochemical applications such as solid-state batteries, where the repeated insertion and removal of ions leaves residual charge in the electrode material. Finally, a broader survey of strongly polar and ionic materials beyond SrTiO
3
 would establish the degree to which these conclusions generalise across the wider space of oxide and halide compounds where long-range electrostatics plays a central role.






