Every protein has a unique stability landscape, but the physical consequences of mutation are governed by recurring biochemical constraints. We test whether a shared decoder, trained on measurements from diverse proteins, can interpret these constraints in an unseen target, enabling cross-protein transfer for initial experimental round prescreening.","Conclusion




Our contribution is a unified representation evaluated across two distinct information regimes. Before any experimental budget is spent, the shared decoder produces a complete wild-type-relative landscape under strict protein holdout, establishing a new state of the art for matched zero-shot stability prediction. As empirical measurements arrive, this exact same landscape accelerates target-specific optimization, surpassing reported Kermut benchmarks without altering base kernels or data splits. By extracting generalized rules of protein stability, 
StabilityArc
 translates these patterns into actionable priors for any new protein engineering campaign.








