2610.00731v1,Measuring Asset and Scene Reconstruction Effects in Real-to-Sim Robot Evaluation,"We test whether our reconstruction pipeline, combining metrically scaled object geometry, authored physical parameters and scene reconstruction, reduces disagreement between simulated and real robot scores relative to a default open-source recipe. We test whether the authored reconstruction narrows the sim-to-real gap in robot performance relative to the default reconstruction.","7 
Conclusion




Simulated evaluation tracks the real robot more closely when the objects and scene are built with metrically scaled geometry and authored physics than when they are built with the default open-source recipe. On one evaluator-operated robot setup with two policies and five tasks, the authored reconstruction reduces the gap to the real robot: mean score error is 6.97 percentage points for the authored reconstruction and 17.54 for the default, a reduction of 10.56 percentage points (95% CI 5.03 to 17.11), and progress disagreement is 15.90 percentage points for the authored reconstruction and 24.23 for the default, a reduction of 8.32 percentage points (1.78 to 16.74). The authored reconstruction is consistent with the real robot’s score in 9 of 10 cells against 5 of 10 for the default, and correlates with real cell means at r = 0.90 against 0.51 for the default. Since the two reconstructions differ in geometry, scale, physics and scene at once, this is the effect of the pipeline as a whole; the effect of the individual factors remains to be established. We release the harness, the paired per-trial scores, every reported run’s configuration, and the assets and scenes of both reconstructions.",cues
