The construction above is a set of Feynman rules for Lindbladians whose
solvable part is an open Gaussian system. Its scope and its limits follow from
the same structural facts.






Essential property.
 The construction rests on the closure of the label
lattice under a small, fixed set of elementary moves. Their algebra is fixed by
the operator basis (with Wick-basis weights set by the unperturbed steady
covariance), while the vertex table for a specified interaction is assembled
mechanically from those moves. It is this assembly rule, rather than one
interaction-independent table of numerical coefficients, that is reused across
problems.
Additivity of the spectrum is a property of one family of propagators, the
Gaussian one; it allows the propagator to be assembled line by line rather
than evaluated on the whole label, and it is a convenience rather than a
condition. In the eigenoperator labels the propagator is always a scalar
(Sec. 
IV.3
); what varies is whether it factorizes over the
lines and how long an expansion a given observable needs.






Within the Gaussian family, Secs. 
