Introduction




One of the most established laws in Physics is the exponential decay law. It is derived at a phenomenological level under the assumption that past events cannot influence the decay rate. Under such conditions, the standard multiexponential description may be replaced by a continuous lifetime distribution as an effective model. This approach is based on the assumption that for each individual decay event the effective decay width is basically constant.","3 
Conclusions




The fluorescence decay data for both erythrosine B and eosine Y can be well described by quantum mechanical models leading to band-dependent power-law behaviour at late times (for details, see Ref. 
[
17
]
). However, this interpretation is not the only possible one, as other mechanisms may also lead to power-law or quasi-power-law behaviour. The analysis presented in this work shows that the specific statistical and energy-migration parametrizations fitted here cannot reproduce the relatively sharp transition from the exponential to the power-law regime observed in the experimental data. Second-order delayed-fluorescence processes can, in principle, generate such a crossover. However, the simple delayed-fluorescence model considered here, based on a single emitting population with a time-independent emission spectrum, does not account for the observed band dependence. Our results provide further support for the interpretation of the observed late-time deviations as a manifestation of nonexponential quantum decay. Further measurements are planned to test this interpretation.








