Conclusion




In this work, we investigated the causal implications of extending the principle of relativity to include superluminal reference-frame transformations. The proposal in 
[
10
]
 shows that tachyons are incompatible with a locally deterministic theory, but it does not establish that a theory containing tachyons becomes consistent once local determinism is abandoned. If we recall Dragan-Ekert’s argument discussed in 
section
 
4
, their reasoning begins by assuming no local description (the past lightcone not having information about emission and absorption in the presence of superluminal reference frames). They then claim that it is necessary to abandon local- 
determinism
 in the presence of superluminal particles, given that there is no-superluminal signalling since we cannot 
control
 the emission and absorption of tachyons or superluminal particles. To us, this reasoning appears circular and lacks the formal definitions and framework needed to clarify the relations between locality, determinism, operational control, and signalling.







Dragan and Ekert appeal to a fundamental form of uncertainty (when they abandon local-determinism), which we call D-E uncertainty and formalise by embedding relevant events in spacetime regions. In this work, we developed a framework in which both the spacetime and the causal model are allowed to depend on the reference frame and communication protocols such as the one considered in 
[
10
]
 can be analysed explicitly.







Within this framework, we proved a no-go theorem demonstrating that the existence of correlations in any reference frame (A0), the existence of superluminal transformations which can change the causal ordering of operational regions (A1), the absence of superluminal signalling (A2), invariance of operational probabilities under reference-frame transformations (A3), and invariance of free variables across reference frames (A4) are inconsistent. The theorem therefore provides a systematic characterisation of the conceptual trade-offs required by theories admitting superluminal reference frames. The theorem identifies this incompatibility but does not determine which assumption should be abandoned or whether every resulting alternative defines a complete and physically satisfactory theory.







Our result also clarifies the limitations of arguments in 
[
10
]
 based solely on objective uncertainty. As discussed in 
section
 
7.2
, D-E uncertainty can evade the no-go theorem only if it sufficiently large that the operational regions remain order-preserving under all admissible reference-frame transformations. This is a substantially stronger requirement than merely postulating indeterminacy, and may imply that agents cannot, even in principle, localise or control communication events and even standard classical scales become unachievable. Thus, the first issue is not whether uncertainty exists, but whether it is sufficiently extensive to preserve the causal ordering of operational regions in every reference frame. Second, even if one accepts that D-E uncertainty may provide one possible way of addressing certain causal paradoxes associated with SpTs, it is neither shown to be sufficient on its own nor uniquely required by the existence of such transformations. Relaxing other assumptions opens alternative routes around the no-go result, which do not necessarily require D-E uncertainty, although their physical viability must be assessed separately. Finally, we also note, D-E uncertainty is specifically a form of spatiotemporal uncertainty concerning the localisation of events in space and time, whereas quantum uncertainty is more general and can apply to other incompatible observables, for example position and momentum.







Our result does not rule out all theories admitting superluminal reference frames; rather, it constrains theories that also seek to preserve the operational principles represented by A0–A4. Our work also demonstrates how causal modelling provides a useful framework for analysing extensions of relativity. By making explicit the assumptions underlying different proposals, it becomes possible to identify precisely where inconsistencies arise and to compare alternative approaches on equal footing. We hope that this framework will prove useful in future investigations of superluminal reference frames, relativistic causality, and more general extensions of spacetime symmetry.",cues
