AEE models share a factorised description of the tile response,
whereas the FEE model relaxes the assumption that every dipole
has the same element pattern.","6. Conclusions
We have developed two complementary Bayesian approaches
for inferring continuous MW A beam patterns from irregular and
incomplete satellite sampling. We first returned to the raw mea-
surements of Chokshi et al. (2021), avoiding the correlations
introduced by their previous interpolation and smoothing, and
derived receiver-specific corrections for the non-linear response
of the RF Explorer spectrum analysers. The reprocessed mea-
surements were then binned onto a HEALPix grid with per-pixel
uncertainties that account for both within-pass noise and variations
between contributing satellite passes.
The S06XX measurements exhibit coherent departures from
both the nominal AEE and FEE predictions, most prominently
through shallower and displaced nulls, a broad depression in the
western sidelobe, and large-scale gradients across the sidelobes.
The dipole model represents much of this deformation using
a compact set of per-dipole gains. Allowing the amplitudes to
vary reduces the residual RMS from 7.889 to 2.945 dB, while
the statistically preferred complex model further reduces it to
2.890 dB and lowers the MAD from 1.188 to 1.015 dB. The
power-only measurements leave two symmetry-related complex-
gain configurations that are physically distinct in parameter space
but predict effectively identical power beams. The inferred gains
should therefore be interpreted as effective descriptions of the
measured deformation rather than unique diagnoses of electronic
gain errors.
The hierarchical spline model provides a complementary
reconstruction relative to the FEE beam. It recovers additional
direction-dependent structure around the nulls and throughout the
sidelobes while generally leaving isolated pass-shaped artefacts
in the residuals. In particular, the spline repeatedly reconstructs
the broad western depression that remains in the residuals of the
dipole model, demonstrating that this feature requires spatial flex-
ibility beyond direction-independent complex gains. Agreement
between the two models identifies structure that is supported under
substantially different parameterisations, while their differences
distinguish what can and cannot be represented within the nominal
AEE geometry.
A central result of this work is that a continuous full-
hemisphere beam pattern can be reconstructed from only 20 days
of satellite observations. Each of four independent 20-day sub-
sets contains approximately 20% of the full dataset and provides a
distinct, incomplete sampling pattern. Nevertheless, every subset
recovers the principal null and sidelobe morphology. Correlations
between the first two spline reconstructions reach 0.95 and 0.96
in the nulls and sidelobes, respectively, while the correspond-
ing dipole corrections have correlations of 0.99 in both regions.
Agreement is weaker in the main lobe, where the measurements
are sparser and more susceptible to residual receiver non-linearity.
Similar behaviour for S10YY (Appendix A) demonstrates that the
reconstruction is not unique to a single tile or polarisation.
The present results remain limited to monochromatic power
beams near 137 MHz. Temporal repeatability also cannot by itself
establish whether a recovered feature originates in the receiving
tile or in a persistent measurement systematic. Extending this
approach to chromatic, phase-referenced voltage measurements
would enable inference of the full Jones response required by
wide-band polarimetric experiments. Nevertheless, the recovery
of consistent full-hemisphere beam patterns from independent 20-
day observing intervals demonstrates that sparse satellite passes
can provide the information required for practical and repeatable
characterisation of deployed receiving elements.
