Conclusions




In this paper, we have shown that an axion field can develop field fluctuations peaked at a characteristic wavenumber. This arises through the time evolution of the radial direction of a symmetry-breaking field during inflation, driven by Hubble-induced masses. For a particular combination of the signs of the Hubble-induced masses, the axion decay constant starts from a large value, decreases, and then increases during inflation. The peak of the axion fluctuation spectrum is set by the moment when the decay constant reaches its minimum, while the IR and UV slopes are controlled by the magnitudes of the Hubble-induced masses.






These axion field perturbations can be converted into curvature perturbations via the curvaton mechanism. We computed the resulting GW and PBH abundances. The GW spectrum has a triangular shape, with IR and UV slopes determined by the Hubble-induced masses.
The NANOGrav signal can be accommodated by our mechanism without overproducing PBHs, once the non-Gaussianity of the curvature perturbations is properly accounted for. If the GW spectrum is measured with greater precision in the future, the model parameters will be further constrained and GW signals at higher frequencies will be predicted.






The PBHs produced in our setup can account for all of dark matter. Regardless of whether they constitute all of dark matter, PBHs in our model generically carry blue-tilted isocurvature perturbations on large scales. CMB constraints require the axion field perturbations to be sufficiently suppressed in the IR, which in turn requires the IR slope of the GW spectrum to be sufficiently steep.






It is worth comparing our mechanism with existing axion curvaton models 
[
80
, 
16
, 
74
, 
60
]
. While these models share the phenomenological success of producing PBHs that can explain dark matter or LIGO events, together with broad and detectable stochastic GW backgrounds, their underlying inflationary dynamics and resulting power spectra differ significantly from ours. Previous studies typically consider scenarios in which the radial field has only a positive Hubble-induced mass and stops evolving once it reaches its minimum. Depending on whether perturbations re-enter the horizon before or after curvaton decay, these models predict fixed UV slopes for the curvature power spectrum (e.g., 
𝒫
