23
, 
26
: the former leads to a parabolic profile, while the latter does not capture the nonlinear origin identified here.
Beyond simple waveguides, the same framework describes complex structures, boundary conditions, and absorption, and reveals how symmetry breaking can reorganize highly transmitting eigenchannels.






The MTE provides an efficient framework for studying wavefront control in structures much larger than the wavelength, without wavelength-scale discretization while retaining the information required for coherent optimization.
This opens the possibility of exploring transmission eigenchannels in complex optical circuits containing random inclusions or defects, and of designing structures with prescribed transmission properties 
32
.
The framework may also be extended to inhomogeneous or anisotropically scattering media, such as biological tissues 
33
, 
34
.






The matrix formulation gives access to observables beyond bulk intensity, including current, energy velocity 
17
, 
35
, and radiance, which are particularly relevant in anisotropic media.
An important extension would be to develop two-point intensity correlations, which could capture phenomena washed out by disorder averaging, such as transverse localization of transmission eigenchannels 
13
.
The framework could likewise be extended to other optimization objectives, such as energy deposition 
36
, 
33
, 
37
, and to time-dependent wavefront control of pulses 
38
, 
39
, 
40
, 
41
.






Several fundamental questions remain open.
One is whether the MTE can be extended to the Anderson localized regime using a self-consistent renormalization approach 
42
.
Another concerns the intrinsic nonlinearity of the MTE: whether the constrained evolution of 
𝖰
\mathsf{Q}
