Conclusions




The quantum Hall effect and topological insulators represent,
respectively, the field-driven and zero-field routes to the same
underlying principle: a bulk topological invariant enforces a protected,
gapless boundary state with a characteristic density of states. Quantum
capacitance, though originally introduced as a device-physics correction
to ordinary parallel-plate capacitance in 2DEGs 
[
17
]
, is well suited
to reading out this DOS structure, because it is a near-equilibrium
electrostatic measurement rather than a transport measurement. In the
QHE, quantum capacitance oscillations confirm the discrete,
macroscopically degenerate Landau-level bulk DOS that underlies the
quantized Hall plateaus 
[
19
]
. In topological insulators, quantum
capacitance measurements have directly extracted the Dirac velocity and
mapped the linear boundary DOS associated with protected surface states,
and have been used to disentangle bulk, depletion, and surface
contributions in real devices 
[
20
, 
21
]
. Pushed further, local
impedance measurements inside the quantum Hall bulk itself, read in
terms of quantum capacitance, show that the textbook picture of a
spatially uniform incompressible bulk is an idealization:
self-consistent screening produces a patchwork of compressible and
incompressible regions that rearranges with magnetic field even while
the Hall plateau stays exactly quantized 
[
24
, 
26
]
—a description
that complements, rather than replaces, the conventional
disorder–localization account of plateau formation 
[
12
, 
13
, 
14
, 
15
]
. The
broader lesson is that experimentally verifying a topological phase
requires probes attuned to what topology actually constrains, and
quantum capacitance, precisely because it measures compressibility
rather than conductance, has become one of the most direct such probes,
both across the field-induced and zero-field families of topological
matter and within the bulk of the quantum Hall system itself. Topology
constrains the existence and protection of the boundary states; quantum
capacitance is what probes their associated density of
states/compressibility.








