This agreement demonstrates that the source-color offset is not introduced by the new inference framework.
Although the measurements show modest site-to-site differences, all three observatories place the source on the same side of the standard color–color relations and reproduce the same qualitative offset.
Thus, the color inconsistency is independently present in the KMTC, KMTA, and KMTS datasets.






Closer inspection of Figure 
10
 shows that the field-star color–color sequence exhibits scatter comparable to the offset of the source from the standard color–color relations.
The discrepancy therefore appears to be a genuine feature of the measured source colors rather than a problem with the underlying data.
The field-star distribution further suggests that real stars in this field exhibit intrinsic color scatter that is not fully captured by the idealized empirical relations or by the current uncertainty model.






Taken together, these tests provide no evidence that the offset is caused by either the marginalized Bayesian methodology or a reduction problem specific to one KMTNet observatory.
Instead, the discrepancy appears to reflect genuine scatter in the measured stellar colors.






A larger sample of jointly observed KMTNet–PRIME events will be required to quantify this additional scatter and determine how it should be propagated into estimates of 
