V.3 
Role of 
gapmoe
 in Two-step Inference




As discussed in Section 
II
, the conventional two-step approach can become problematic when higher-order effects are weak.
Our main framework avoids this issue by incorporating the Galactic prior directly into the light-curve inference.






The usefulness of 
gapmoe
, however, is not limited to this direct-inference framework.
As discussed in Section 
II.3
, the second-stage inference can become highly inefficient when only a small fraction of samples drawn from the Galactic prior are consistent with the observational constraints.
Because 
gapmoe
 allows the Galactic prior density to be evaluated directly at arbitrary physical parameters, the second-stage inference can instead be formulated as an MCMC problem, so that the sampler can directly explore regions of high posterior probability without relying on rejection sampling.






Therefore, 
gapmoe
 is useful not only for incorporating a Galactic prior directly into light-curve inference, but also as a density model that improves Galactic-prior-based inference within a two-step framework.
This advantage may be particularly important for catalog-level statistical analyses based on large surveys.










VI 
Summary and Future Prospects




VI.1 
Summary




We clarified the limitations of the conventional two-step Bayesian analysis of binary-lens events.
When higher-order effects such as microlensing parallax and lens orbital motion are included, an apparently uninformative prior in the light-curve parameter space can introduce significant biases in the inferred physical parameters, and this effect can remain non-negligible even when the higher-order signals are moderately constrained (Section 
II
).
The same prior can also drive MCMC sampling toward physically implausible regions while undersampling the physically relevant ones favored by a Galactic prior.
As a result, posterior reweighting becomes highly inefficient and may fail to recover the physical posterior accurately when the relevant regions are poorly sampled.






To address this, we introduced a physically consistent Bayesian framework that directly incorporates a Galactic prior during sampling in light-curve parameters such as 
t
E
t_{\rm E}
.
This was made possible by two technical developments: (1) accurate computation of the full Jacobian 
