We present a proprioceptive dexterous grasping policy that acquires
and maintains grasps from hand-local observations and can be paired
with independently designed arm controllers. A bidirectional
arm–hand interface uses the learned grasp score to coordinate
the transition to manipulation and recovery from failed grasps.
We evaluate grasping performance across diverse simulated objects
and show that the policy remains effective under conditions not
encountered during training. Qualitative hardware demonstrations
show successful grasping of 29 real-world objects and reuse of the
same frozen hand policy with three arm behaviors not seen during
training. We also demonstrate reuse across different arm mounting
configurations and robot platforms. Together, these results suggest
that dexterous grasping can be treated as a reusable hand-level skill
rather than being tied to a particular arm controller or perception
pipeline.








